inline_comments.body:
Fixed in 7ae21b195e.

New coverage:

- p = 0.99, 0.995 and 0.999 in every regime;
- a `wide` regime (range above the 40-nat cap, so the capped last bin is exercised);
- a `shifted` regime (logits around 300);
- a padded stride with a finite `mask_value`;
- signed-zero ties;
- gather overflow, exact on ties, and the bounded mode-2 superset past the distinct-value budget;
- the CUDA-graph replay, now checked against the float64 reference.

The rounding allowance is now stated precisely: the kept set must be a prefix of the reference order, and any differing tokens must lie where the float64 cumulative mass is within 1e-6 of p. At p = 0.999 the tail tokens are smaller than fp32 resolution, so several of them can sit inside that band. "One token" was the wrong rule.

Results: 78 passed on this revision. With the same tests, the previous head fails 33, including every high-p regime at V = 200064 and the signed-zero test.

Partially fixed in 7ae21b195e.

Fixed:

- the row-state allocation uses `_RS`;
- the `(max, min)` stats and `(bits, index)` fine-pair strides are named where the scratch is sized;
- the cache key is gone with the grow-only workspace;
- workspace and warmup dtypes come from one table.

I kept `_num_slices` separate from `_topp_split_count`, because they implement different policies. The histogram passes need slices of at least 4096 tokens to amortize a 4096-bin histogram per program, and they stop at about 4 programs per SM. The split pipeline keeps splitting while slices stay at least 7168 tokens, whatever the batch size. Merging them would mean parameterizing both thresholds and both stop conditions, which would not leave much shared code.
